 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186143544 1071 "![](images/69/6948173f453895d0dd62a047aa1476c7d537a84962f51c43a3e0da70949e1a5e.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{7}} = 7$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186143984 1064 "Mixed numbers are a common occurrence in everyday life. Therefore, we can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting mixed numbers to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniela is sewing a costume. She needs\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nyards of gold fabric and\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nyards of purple fabric. How many yards of fabric does she need to make her costume?\n\nTo find the total yards of fabric that Daniela needs, we would need to add\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of gold fabric) to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of purple fabric):\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+1 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ already has a denominator of $6$.\n- To put $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}} = 3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniela needs\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nyards of fabric to make her costume." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186143984 1064 "Mixed numbers are a common occurrence in everyday life. Therefore, we can apply our knowledge of adding and subtracting mixed numbers to real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniela is sewing a costume. She needs\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nyards of gold fabric and\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nyards of purple fabric. How many yards of fabric does she need to make her costume?\n\nTo find the total yards of fabric that Daniela needs, we would need to add\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of gold fabric) to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n(the number of yards of purple fabric):\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+1 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ already has a denominator of $6$.\n- To put $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac1