\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -3\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,\n$$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n\\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186147232 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coeffici