 17592186576411 true] [17592186157030 1074 "To decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(13,55)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=13$ modulo $n=55$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}52^{13}≡17 & (mod55) \\\\ 49^{13}≡4 & (mod55) \\\\ 53^{13}≡3 & (mod55)\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:$\\color{blue}17$$\\color{blue}4$$\\color{blue}3$RED\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$R \\:\\: E \\:\\: D$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186157031 1074 "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 &= \\begin{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}6 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 7 &= \\end{vmatrix}6 \\cdot 1 \\mid + \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:20)$, which means that the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $20$ is $e^{-1}=3$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (3,33)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(3,33)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=3$ modulo $n=33$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15^{3}≡09 & (mod33) \\\\ 16^{3}≡04 & (mod33) \\\\ 13^{3}≡19 & (mod33)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:$\\color{blue}09$$\\color{blue}04$$\\color{blue}19$JET\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$J \\:\\: E \\:\\: T$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186157031 1074 "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 &= \\begin{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}6 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 7 &= \\end{vmatrix}6 \\cdot 1 \\mid + \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod20) 