igma}^2$ is our estimate for the variance $\\sigma^2$,$N$ is the total number of observations, $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ is the midpoint of an observational class $(a_i, b_i)$.\n\nLet's compute estimates for the mean and variance in this case:\n\nThe total number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 8+12+11+9= 40\n$$\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of each observational class.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ | $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 3$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $3\\leq y_i < 5$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 7$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n| $7\\leq y_i < 9$ | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nOur estimate $\\widehat{\\mu}$ for the mean $\\mu$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{μ} &= \\frac{1}{N}∑O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{40}(8 \\cdot 2 + 12 \\cdot 4 + 11 \\cdot 6 + 9 \\cdot 8) \\\\\n&= 5.05\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{N - 1}} \\sum  {O}_{i} \\cdot  ( {y}_{i}^{\\text{mid}} - \\hat{\\mu } {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{40 - 1}} ( 8 \\cdot  ( 2 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 12 \\cdot  ( 4 - 5.05 {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} + 11 \\cdot  ( 6 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 9 \\cdot  ( 8 - 5.05 {)}^{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.51025 \\ldots  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}4.5}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWhen conducting a chi-square test, we lose a degree of freedom for every estimated parameter:\n\n- If *only one* parameter must be estimated, then we have two constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 2$.\n- If *both* parameters must be estimated, then we have three constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 3$.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139539078 17592186576411 tru