 the function is decreasing on $x \\in (2,4)$.\n\n![](images/44/44344c22138c44ddc45e3ef0fbe0b54e3d47f9e6c5288749b3caa9f3e1a5b2a2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. To find the derivative, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,}\n$$ option. Then, we need to enter the point at which to compute the derivative. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=3$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -0.061\\,931$.\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $f'(3) \\approx -0.062$.\n- Statement III is true. To find the integral, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\,}\n$$ option. Then, we select the lower limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=2$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Next, we select the upper limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=4$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 0.392\\,602.\n$$\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.393.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166481 1074 "We can plot the graph of $y=f(x)$ using a graphing calculator, zooming or adjusting the window ranges for each statement to get a better view of the local behavior.\n\nIn this case, appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-1,5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-1,1]$\n\nThis gives the graph below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf59a94ae74182b8e4fa60f50ddcfbf23e5c4041c901789effddf4734d0853be.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph below shows that the function is decreasing on $x \\in (2,4)$.\n\n![](images/44/44344c22138c44ddc45e3ef0fbe0b54e3d47f9e6c5288749b3caa9f3e1a5b2a2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. To find the derivative, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,}$ option. Then, we need to enter the point at which to compute the derivative. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=3$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -0.061\\,931$.\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $f'(3) \\approx -0.062$.\n- Statement III is true. To find the integral, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\,}$ option. Then, we select the lower limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=2$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Next, we select the upper limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=4$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 0.392\\,602$.\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.393$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166483 1074 "Evaluating our function at the point $x=0.7$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(0.7\\right)=\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln(0.7)} \\right) \\approx -0.973\\,0\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.7$.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button first.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\nf'\\left(0.7\\right) = \\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln x} \\right) \\right) \\right|_{x=0.7} \\approx 2.591\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2.591\\,5$ and the coordinates of our point\n\n$$\n\\left(0.7, -0.973\\,0\\right)\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-0.9730) &= 2.5915(x - 0.7) \\\\\ny + 0.9730 &= 2.5915x - 1.8141 \\\\\ny &= 2.5915x - 2.7871\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny = 2.6x - 2.8\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166483 1074 "Evaluating our function at the point $x=0.7$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(0.7\\right)=\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln(0.7)} \\right) \\approx -0.973\\,0\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.7$.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}$ button. You may need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button first.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\nf'\\left(0.7\\right) = \\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln x} \\right) \\right) \\right|_{x=0.7}